(60, NULL, 'DRA. EULALIA ELGAR CLINIC MANAGEMENT SYSTEM', 'The emergence of technological advancements provided innovation in organizations to efficiently accomplished tasks. The idea is to eliminate the manual method of doing transactions manually, and saving time in the process of appointment. This study generally aims to develop a web-based Dra. Eulalia Elgar Clinic Management System for Espiritu-Elgar Medical Clinic, with these this study was conducted to develop appointment clinic management system that would systematically organize the appointment scheduling of patients/client, to manage user account, manage patient record, generate the report and generate the data analytics. This study used a mixed-methods research design that combines parts of quantitative and qualitative research to contextualize the data, therefore only the data gathered from the target respondents would be the only references for the development of the proposed system. After the collection of data through an interview with a guide questions, analysis was done using the frequency percentage and weighted mean. The iterative method was utilized throughout the study. On the system development, the result shows that the most frequently encountered problems in the clinic are the patient queue and the manual process of recording patients’ information. Furthermore, the evaluation of the performance of the system, the result shows 1 in level of functionality, and the respondents agree that the functionality of the develop system was excellent. The researchers concluded that clinic management system is necessary in managing clinical processes, the system development is effectively being done by following modules and phases of an Iterative Model, with the use of Black Box Testing. Based from a forementioned conclusions, researchers arrived with the following recommendations that future researchers conduct studies in other relevant research environments, consider document retrieval and document security consider and add billing statement in other related studies for further enhancement of the study.', 'JONNABEL M. AWA, KHRYZ KYE B. BADIOLA, MA. LOURDES D. IBARRETA, ROCHEL F. TABERMEJO', '[-0.8963065147399902, 1.1172497272491455, -2.961537599563598, -1.0187125205993652, 0.17963723838329315, -0.53280109167099, 0.3437039256095886, 0.19734525680541992, 0.7370584607124329, -0.1256605088710785, 0.7203259468078613, -0.7840531468391418, 3.239016532897949, -0.12156584113836288, 0.06375009566545486, -0.28569114208221436, -0.16820397973060608, -0.5419373512268066, -1.472931146621704, 0.24280965328216553, 0.21501752734184265, -0.8465805053710938, -0.8749352097511292, -0.2257969230413437, 1.476562261581421, 0.31214651465415955, 0.364928662776947, 0.8990265727043152, -0.4965965449810028, -0.029550183564424515, 1.458425998687744, -0.4098600149154663, 0.4403649866580963, -0.19164703786373136, -0.3581392168998718, -1.0252883434295654, 0.3125445246696472, -0.2098804712295532, 0.20980456471443176, 0.36796483397483826, 0.5140615701675415, -0.7943931818008423, 0.07510578632354736, -0.9871144890785216, 0.48827677965164185, -0.11615703999996184, 1.4040942192077637, 0.7232474088668823, 0.1925005465745926, -0.13568788766860962, 0.7522291541099548, -0.00967094674706459, -0.42438966035842896, 0.6478650569915771, 0.1612338274717331, 0.6712170243263245, 0.4457817077636719, 1.0535250902175903, -0.03183332458138466, -1.6127766370773315, 1.9287983179092407, 0.9870308041572572, -1.3483558893203735, 1.1546553373336792, 0.7996630668640137, -0.4046098887920379, 0.38449805974960327, 0.6967114210128784, 0.7016459703445435, -0.90632426738739, -0.2492633759975433, -0.282793790102005, 0.1586882621049881, -0.3688712120056152, 0.49554023146629333, 0.4141542911529541, -0.773815393447876, -0.43657127022743225, -0.03932825475931168, -0.22114557027816772, 1.0358843803405762, -0.1825481355190277, 1.9961110353469849, -0.31560078263282776, 0.9331766366958618, -0.1670292615890503, -0.33378446102142334, -0.22206