(90, NULL, 'ELOCK: A MULTI-FACTOR AUTHENTICATION UTILIZING FINGERPRINT RECOGNITION AND PIN CODE IDENTIFICATION', 'The CCS NAS Laboratory previously relied on a manual door lock system and logbook monitor- ing, which posed significant challenges in terms of security, efficiency, and reliability. To address these issues, the researchers developed eLock, an automated smart door lock system that integrates biometric fingerprint authentication, PIN code verification, and real-time access monitoring through a web-based interface. The system enhances laboratory security by ensuring that only authorized personnel can access the facility while automating access logs for better record-keeping and trace- ability. In the Agile software development methodology, during the analysis phase the clients en- gaged themselves in reviewing documents and interviews done in order to enhance both functional and non-functional requirements. This approach therefore helped in positioning system features to effectively address client requirements, accommodate changes in requirements in real sense and also reduce consumption of resources and time. The study also also used the black box testing techniques to determine the effectiveness, feasibility, security, and dependability of the developed system. It was observed from the results that eLock recorded a success rate of all the test cases and that the score for the functionality was recorded to be 0.97 which describes the technical interpreta- tion as “Completely Functional”. It was fully able to achieve quality access control, proper handling of user data, and high throughput even when it was under heavy load. Through implementing cru- cial processes and boosting security features the eLock system effectively solved all the laboratory’s operational issues and increased the general dependability and openness of the facility.', 'JOSEPH E. LANZUELA, MAREALIE C. ESPEDILLON, VINZ CHRISTIAN T. MARGATE', '[-0.252946138381958, 0.7110267281532288, -3.2