(111, NULL, 'SECURITECH: MODERNIZING DOOR LOCK SYSTEM WITH RFID AND SENSOR IN LIS LABORATORY', 'The LIS Laboratory in ter the College of Compu Studies faces challenges in securing valuable equipment and displaying data due to the traditional manual locking system. Issues such as lost or duplicate keys, unauthorized access, and inefficient manual operations compromise securit and disrupt productivity. In addition, manual unlocking and monitoring of doors affects the functioning of teachers and students To address these problems, this stud developed SecuriTech, a modernized door lock system that enhances access control and security of the laboratory. The Extreme Programming (XP) methodology, which emphasizes iterativ development and user collaboration. A mixed-method approach combining quantitative and qualitative research was used. Purposiv and convenience sampling were used to select respondents. Unit testing verified the functionality of individua components of the RFID module, ultrasonic sensor, and alarm system, while black box testing ensured the overall functionality of the system. Integration Testing proved the seamless interactio between hardware an software components, while Stress Testing demonstrated stability under differen workloads. Securi testing evaluated protection against access, improve attendance tracking, and enhance the user experience, offering a significant advancement in educational laboratory management and serving as a model for innovative and secure access control solutions in educational institutions.', 'MONICA G. BONDOY, ED JIM C. BUQUID, RALPH DEXTER RHEY A. PIDOC', '[-0.7169537544250488, 0.48633766174316406, -3.343144655227661, -0.3922650218009949, 1.5823078155517578, -0.36240774393081665, 0.7058720588684082, 0.811152458190918, 0.4875471591949463, -0.09353183209896088, -0.35542190074920654, -0.12741100788116455, 0.9789987206459044, 0.37511375546455383, 0.05291515588760376, -0.21289603412151337, 0.7703990340232849, -0.8287601470947266, 