(119, NULL, 'Constraint Satisfaction Problem (CSP) Algorithm in Class Scheduling', 'The integration of Constraint Satisfaction Problems (CSPs) into class scheduling optimizes educational resources like classrooms, faculty, and time slots. The CSP algorithm ensures minimal con-flicts and smooth student enrollment by addressing constraints such as faculty availability and course requirements, while enforcing time constraints and accommodating special requirements like lab-oratories and instructor availability. User-friendly tools empower administrators for accurate and efficient schedule creation and management. The system demonstrates efficiency, adaptability, and stability through CPU usage analysis, revealing iterative improve-ments in resource utilization. Fluctuations in CPU usage, initially high at 1.5 Mb and decreasing to 0.5 Mb in subsequent iterations, indicate optimized computations. Periodic variations offer insights for algorithm refinement. Speed evaluation in Figure 11 illustrates consistent 3-second durations in initial iterations, suggesting sta-bility, with a reduction to 2 seconds by the 3rd iteration, indicating optimization. Iterations 4 to 10 maintain this efficiency, balanc-ing complexity and resource utilization. However, a temporary increase to 3 seconds at iteration 11 suggests additional processing requirements, subsequently returning to 2 seconds, with fluctua-tions reflecting adaptive behavior based on computational needs. Analyzing these patterns provides insights into system efficiency and adaptability, facilitating algorithm refinement to handle diverse computational challenges.', 'Julie A. Sandrino, John Leonard P. Donio, John Mark P. Peñales, Karl Kennedy J. Mata', '[-0.06219358742237091, 1.9243395328521729, -3.0701448917388916, -0.7359409332275391, 0.15538805723190308, -0.4676667451858521, 0.9684299230575562, -0.20563167333602905, -0.46183496713638306, 0.27728071808815, 0.20701399445533752, 0.15559664368629456, 1.4824137687683103, 0.9