(121, NULL, 'Mobile Augmented Reality For Learning Computer Hardware Using Simultaneous Localization and Mapping', 'Augmented reality is an emerging technology applied in var-ious fields such as education. One significant gap is the lack of comprehensive educational content, AR experience and interactivity in existing AR applications for computer hard-ware learning. Many available applications provide basic information but fail to engage users in meaningful interac-tions with virtual components. With this, the researchers developed a mobile AR application that enabled users to vi-developed a mobile AR application that enabled users to vi-sualize and interact with computer hardware components in real-world environments. The system utilized SLAM al-gorithms that accurately mapped the physical space and tracked the device\'s position and orientation in real-time. This allowed seamless integration of virtual computer hard-ware models into the user\'s surroundings, created a mixed-reality learning experience. The method section outlined the design principles and development process of the proposed application. The researchers conducted a survey and content validity was evaluated in terms of Functionality Suitability, Performance Efficiency and Usability using rating scale. The respondents were CSPC-CCS faculty and students taking Operating System course. The evaluation test result showed that the content is good with an overall Average Mean of 3.29, therefore it is connected to the student\'s curriculum. The developed application is highly usable as a mobile learning application very much evident with an Average Mean of 3.31 in the system as perceived by the two groups of respondents', 'Jazmine Claire B. Bernardino, Angelica Lagsic, Lyndsy D. Villanueva', '[-0.5881640911102295, 0.8787420988082886, -2.6638197898864746, -0.5457900166511536, 0.07413318008184433, 1.505197048187256, 1.4187514781951904, -0.3000349700450897, 0.4990246891975403, 0.5693130493164062, 0.92975914478