(134, NULL, 'Sentiment Analysis of Covid-19 Tweets Using Machine Learning', 'COVID-19 is the world\'s most serious problem. Twitter is a social networking service where users may share their thoughts and opin- ions. Sentiment analysis is the study of subjective information in an expression; it can be classed as positive, negative, or neutral emotions that people have about a topic. The researchers used their findings to undertake a sentiment analysis of English tweets during the COVID-19 epidemic in 2020. The goal of this research is to establish whether the highest and lowest values are classified as negative. neutral, or positive. Machine learning is a technique for discovering patterns in massive amounts of data and increasing the accuracy and classification of each data point without the need for explicit programming. The data was acquired from 12,227 English- language tweets from 2020 using the Twitter scraping tool twint, which was taken from Twitter via the application programming interface along with additional useful information. The algorithm classifier generated the following total rate: Only 28.1% of the data was identified as negative, with 35.3% and 36.6% identified as neu- tral and positive sentiment, respectively. The accuracy rate of the performance classifier for the support vector machine is 89.85%. In conclusion, the sentiment analysis of the data revealed that positive and neutral attitudes had more categories than negative attitudes, which had fewer. This is critical to the analysis\'s accuracy since the more labeled data there is, the more precise the results will be.', 'John Lloyd G. Lavarias, Jeuness E. Buena, Jessebel A. Bueno, Shane P. Laya', '[0.7320916652679443, 0.293024480342865, -3.3859057426452637, -0.8743343949317932, 1.4751334190368652, 0.516507089138031, 0.4419212341308594, 0.12866593897342682, -0.07525637745857239, 0.3635833263397217, 0.398509293794632, 0.34675246477127075, 1.4127947092056274, 0.9196861386299132, -0.54577994346618