(147, NULL, 'REAL-TIME LOCAL WEATHER STATION USING GRADIENT BOOSTING', 'This study presents a Real-Time Local Weather Station prototype utilizing the Gradient Boosting algorithm to enhance weather pattern analysis and prediction. The system addresses the limitations of traditional statistical methods by leveraging machine learning to analyze complex meteorological data and improve the accuracy of forecasts. By processing key parameters such as temperature, wind speed, humidity, and rainfall, the prototype provides valuable insights into local weather dynamics, enabling timely and informed decision-making. Using localized data from Project Apaw, the system integrates real-time data analysis with a web-based dashboard for visualization, offering an accessible platform for stakeholders to monitor and analyze weather conditions. The model\'s perfor- mance was evaluated using metrics such as accuracy, precision, F1-score, MAE, and RMSE, demonstrating strong reliability, par- ticularly in predicting rainfall with 98% accuracy and precision. Exploratory Data Analysis also revealed key correlations between meteorological variables, guiding feature selection and improving the model\'s predictive capabilities. While challenges such as data quality, resource constraints, and system integration remain, the study highlights the potential of machine learning in advancing localized weather forecasting. The prototype contributes to disaster preparedness, resource management, and climate resilience by providing accurate and actionable weather insights. This research emphasizes the critical role of advanced predictive modeling in transforming weather monitoring and decision-making.', 'Johnlery E. Mirandilla, Jomar P. Damot, Vincent James O. Salvino', '[0.174168199300766, -0.22173058986663816, -3.897196769714355, -1.1564476490020752, 0.7199150323867798, 0.32905006408691406, 1.2962496280670166, 0.49130749702453613, 0.2905571460723877, -0.08452823013067245, 0.2447427213191986, 0.1507815