(164, NULL, 'Integration of Robotic Process Automation (RPA) 1.0 in the Academic Operational Tasks of the College of Computer Studies (CCS): A Module Focusing on Adviser’s Reportorial Requirements', 'Automation made processes more accurate and efficient, making operations smoother and increasing industry productivity. This study explored the integration of Robotic Process Automation (RPA) 1.0 in the academic operational tasks of the College of Computer Studies (CCS), focusing on automating adviser reportorial requirements. The main objective was to understand the existing step-by-step procedures for preparing and generating the adviser’s reportorial requirements such as delinquency, top 10 lists, dean’s and president’s lists, and assessing its automatability. Creating business process models and workflows, assessing automation, automating tasks, and evaluating benefits in terms of accuracy and efficiency. The study introduced a unique RPA 1.0 lifecycle model, adapting traditional Business Process Management (BPM) methods to academic workflows, resulting in significant benefits. Automation saved 1 hour, 4 minutes, and 52 seconds in reportorial tasks, boosting productivity, and achieved 100% accuracy, according to all respondents. Researchers employed a qualitative approach, which allowed for a comprehensive understanding of the problems that advisers encounter while preparing reports. Purposive sampling was used in the study to ensure that participants were selected proportionately to the intended scope and special features. The data was gathered through interviews, observations, and inspections. The findings revealed high automation potential for tasks like report creation and data validation. The implementation of RPA 1.0 successfully automated report generation using Microsoft Power Automate, but despite these improvements, challenges such as complex tasks, security concerns, and dependency on internet connectivity were identified. The study concluded that RPA significantly improved efficiency and accuracy in academic operations and recommends continued updates and maintenance for long-term efficiency.', 'IVY ESPAÑOLA, MICHAEL LAURAYA, TREISHCIA MEI I. NACARIO', '[0.33676841855049133, 0.3508145809173584, -2.7399744987487793, -0.4973278045654297, 0.6579207181930542, -0.28583192825317383, 1.1370922327041626, 0.158861443400383, 0.13163594901561737, 0.2720651626586914, 0.0921458974480629, -0.19518247246742249, 1.6246556043624878, 0.11061680316925047, 0.22514483332633972, -0.7182976007461548, -0.08552776277065277, -0.5294210910797119, -0.9990639686584472, 0.5227524638175964, 0.8363000750541687, -1.493957757949829, -0.05743313580751419, -0.24186718463897705, 1.8404457569122312, 0.9548981189727784, -0.35198867321014404, -1.302868127822876, -0.11808723956346512, -0.1499510109424591, 0.7991323471069336, -0.22143104672431943, -0.38290420174598694, -0.9053564071655272, -0.5893814563751221, -1.3177506923675537, 1.4658368825912476, -0.6354964971542358, 0.7455670833587646, 0.8610244989395142, 0.007602454163134098, -0.36657795310020447, 0.7885841131210327, -0.6270027160644531, 0.4807575345039368, 0.42415091395378113, 0.92839378118515, -0.5382391214370728, 1.3228470087051392, 0.0042668236419558525, 0.747178316116333, -0.3466072678565979, -0.6946787238121033, 0.3734875619411469, 0.9510950446128844, 0.36001452803611755, 0.007469471544027328, 0.9289059638977052, 0.22689130902290344, -1.3422414064407349, 1.4711081981658936, 1.582535982131958, -0.1661222279071808, 1.3274061679840088, 0.6504629850387573, -0.23071856796741488, 1.3711135387420654, 0.5094586610794067, -0.4103107154369354, 0.11606742441654204, -0.42085856199264526, 0.011391530744731426, -0.1241927444934845, 0.3348389267921448, -0.30515027046203613, 0.28513097763061523, -0.5243008136749268, -0.6897339224815369, -0.6129636764526367, 1.4617615938186646, 0.4787666201591492, -0.16921089589595795, 1.454411268234253, 1.1408859491348269, 0.4391307830810547, -0.4133795201778412, 0.03085507079