(167, NULL, 'Impact Assessment of Records Management System in CSPC: A Delone and Mclean Analysis', 'An effective Records Management System (RMS) is critical for organizational accountability, transparency, and efficiency. This study evaluated the RMS at Camarines Sur Polytechnic Colleges (CSPC) using the Delone and McLean Success Model, focusing on system quality, information quality, service quality, intention to use, user satisfaction, and net benefits. A mixed-methods approach was employed, gathering data through surveys and interviews with administrators, basic users, and IT experts. Results were analyzed using a weighted mean to assess perceptions based on the six dimensions. Findings revealed strong system usability (4.25) and reliability (4.03) but weaker efficiency (3.14) and flexibility (3.93), highlighting the need for faster processing and adaptability. Information quality was high in accuracy (4.09), completeness (4.03), and relevance (4.01) but required improvement in timeliness (3.99). Service quality excelled in empathy (4.16) and assurance (4.08) but showed lower responsiveness (3.58) and reliability (3.66). User satisfaction was positive, contributing to the intention to continue using the system. Net benefits reflected the RMS\'s role in enhancing organizational efficiency and decision-making. Identified areas for improvements include adding barcode scanning, dropdown menus, tooltips, and document classification. Suggestions also focus on removing resigned users, updating directories, enabling real-time notifications, conducting workshops, and ensuring continuous updates. These recommendations suggest future efforts to optimize tracking and indexing processes, refine system functionalities, and develop an improved version to further enhance organizational outcomes.', 'JOSHUA B. DE LOS REYES, JERICO R. LLENO, JOHN ANTHONY L. VILLANUEVA', '[-0.894929826259613, 0.2858179807662964, -3.1193089485168457, -0.7227532267570496, 0.7397605776786804, -0.71721