(173, NULL, 'Robotic Process Automation (RPA) 1.0 in the Academic Operations of the College of Computer Studies (CCS): A Module on Student Retention and Sustained Academic Performance Assessment', 'Robotic Process Automation (RPA) is an emerging technology designed to automate repetitive tasks, therefore helping organizations improve efficiency, accuracy, and overall productivity while reducing human error. Despite the existing digital systems at Camarines Sur Polytechnic Colleges (CSPC), several academic operations in the College of Computer Studies (CCS) remain manually executed, resulting in process delays and inconsistencies. These circumstances motivated the conduct of this study, aiming to integrate Robotic Process Automation (RPA) 1.0 in the generation of student retention and sustained academic performance reports. Specifically, the study identified existing manual processes, assessed their automatability, and evaluated the performance of the automated system. The study utilized the Business Process Management Lifecycle and adopted a multi-method research approach. Data was gathered through interviews, observations, and analysis of academic records. The findings showed that several tasks in the student retention and sustained academic performance workflows were classified as having a high degree of automation and fell under the high- value, low-skill quadrant, making them top priorities for automation. Moreover, the integration of RPA 1.0 using Microsoft Power Automate Desktop minimized human errors and reduced the report generation time by an average of 1 hour and 32 seconds for student retention reports and 26 minutes and 44 seconds for sustained academic performance reports, while ensuring 100% accuracy. The redesigned automated process proved to be more efficient and reliable compared to the manual system. Therefore, the implementation of Robotic Process Automation (RPA) 1.0 is recommended to enhance workflow efficiency and support the digital transformation initiatives of CSPC.', 'NICOLE JEANIE LL. ABONITA, MARY KRISTINE MAE R. LARESMA, NOEL RICHARD E. TUBIG', '[0.1551920473575592, 0.2839704751968384, -2.552748680114746, -0.2881063222885132, 0.9107578992843628, 0.3825918436050415, 1.4199098348617554, 0.09657643735408784, 0.5589649081230164, 0.5865139365196228, 0.22187179327011108, -0.08331533521413803, 1.6031805276870728, 0.09015807509422302, 0.1913406252861023, -1.231794357299805, 0.05137450248003006, -0.5552294254302979, -1.1702394485473633, 0.45282626152038574, 0.8468078374862671, -1.5604228973388672, 0.2458638697862625, -0.09609461575746536, 2.0474400520324707, 1.0957626104354858, -0.7354182004928589, -1.1992106437683103, -0.1594916135072708, -0.11481241136789322, 0.5311021208763123, -0.17707231640815735, -0.1912255883216858, -0.6596051454544067, -0.5167197585105896, -0.7852637767791748, 1.608510971069336, -1.1311079263687134, 0.5896057486534119, 0.9084241390228271, -0.2988757789134979, -0.5004879236221313, 0.7160150408744812, -0.3832070231437683, 0.5545411705970764, 0.4797896146774292, 0.4118008315563202, -0.6995171904563904, 1.2198830842971802, -0.017522208392620087, 0.7966120839118958, -0.18982870876789093, -0.8469828963279724, 0.3414723873138428, 0.5456233024597168, 0.22086447477340695, -0.4387746453285218, 0.7702084183692932, 0.09753040969371796, -1.579577922821045, 1.2910975217819214, 2.0546765327453613, -0.5396541357040405, 1.2879066467285156, 0.6406481862068176, -0.2939075529575348, 1.0799556970596311, 0.3429005742073059, -0.35783571004867554, 0.09094440937042236, -0.25545164942741394, -0.1505052000284195, 0.3029565215110779, 0.5714972019195557, -0.3341318964958191, -0.23473945260047913, -0.7787067890167236, -0.8705155849456787, -0.506058931350708, 1.5750951766967771, 0.24998168647289276, -0.147038996219635, 1.3335493803024292, 1.426586389541626, 0.5236400961875916, -0.4664994180202484, -0.27899372577667236, -0.5372815132141113, -0.3709707260131836, 1.318982481956482, 0.47358810901641846, 0.60871100425