(178, NULL, 'CSPC LIBRARY PROCESS AUTOMATION IN INFOLIB AND KOHA USING RPA', 'The growing need for efficiency in academic libraries has encouraged institutions to adopt automation tools that improve precision and processing speed. This study integrates Robotic Process Automation (RPA) through Microsoft Power Automate Desktop (PAD) to automate selected routines in the Camarines Sur Polytechnic Colleges Library, specifically within the INFOLIB and KOHA systems. Guided by the Business Process Management (BPM) framework, the existing manual procedures were analyzed, their automatability assessed, and redesigned into automated to-be processes evaluated through accuracy and time-efficiency metrics. Automation focused on tasks involving bibliographic record entry in INFOLIB and overdue report generation in KOHA, processes that are highly repetitive, rule-based, and suitable for automation. Automatability was examined using value-complexity analysis and color-coded matrices, confirming that these tasks met the criteria for RPA integration. The redesigned process models were transformed into executable workflows, enabling PAD to replicate the manual steps consistently and reliably. Results showed that the automated procedures achieved 100% accuracy in both bibliographic processing and circulation- related outputs. Time efficiency also improved significantly, with processing durations reduced by 5.11 seconds in INFOLIB and 50.66 seconds in KOHA compared to manual execution. These findings demonstrate that RPA effectively reduces staff workload, minimizes human error, and enhances operational stability in academic library environments. The study concludes that automation through PAD can serve as a practical model for modernizing library workflows and supporting digital transformation. Future research may explore automating additional library operations, improving evaluation metrics, and conducting broader testing to strengthen applicability.', 'JHOSUA O. BENOSA, MARK REGIE E. 