(190, NULL, 'Integration of Robotic Process Automation (RPA) 1.0 in the Academic Operational Tasks of the College of Computer Studies (CCS): A Module Automating the Procedure for Generation of Workload Schedule of Faculty', 'Employees in moder workplaces increasingly rely on technology to manage operational processes yet requires proper planning and implementation to remain efficient. In the College of Computer Studies (CCS) at Camarines Sur Polytechnic Colleges, several long-standing procedures particularly in the manual preparation of documents have become repetitive, and time-consuming. This study, The Integration of Robotic Process Automation (RPA) to the Academic Operational Tasks of the College of Computer Studies, aims to enhance the Generation of Workload Schedule of the Faculty by assessing its automation potential and effectiveness. A multi-method approach was used to meet the study\'s objectives. Qualitative methods, including interviews and observations, identified workflow patterns and faculty concerns, while quantitative methods provided numerical interpretations through scoring formulas, data inspection, and simulation. Guided by the Business Process Management (BPM) Framework, the researchers (1) analyzed the existing workflow mapped in As-Is BPMN model, (2) evaluated automatability using Multi-Criteria Scoring Model and Value-Complexity Matrix, and (3) developed a To-Be Model Nineteen tasks were identified, with seventeen (89.47%) classified as highly automatable and two (10.53%) as medium. Four tasks were deemed highly complex due to complex plotting requirements. The redesigned automated workflow implemented Microsoft Power Automate Desktop in VirtualBox showing a reduction in processing time from an average of 4 minutes and 60 seconds manually to 37 seconds with automation, achieving 100% accuracy across ten generated documents. The study concludes that RPA 1.0 significantly improves efficiency and reliability and recommends exploring more advanced RPA versions, to other departments, and refining workflows for future research.', 'NIÑO JAYBEE R. BERMAS, RUBY MAE O. PENOLIO, ADRIAN G. SABALLERO', '[0.08998005837202072, 0.7017778158187866, -2.5431807041168213, -0.48663267493247986, 0.694959282875061, -0.28812843561172485, 1.2276370525360107, -0.2566739618778229, -0.06014556810259819, 0.3898979723453522, 0.30332472920417786, -0.3645079433917999, 1.5830549001693726, 0.16875973343849182, -0.11539436131715776, -1.140089511871338, 0.03343387693166733, -0.40361618995666504, -1.14505934715271, 0.3056407570838928, 0.5186536312103271, -1.245100498199463, 0.4450444281101227, -0.22185197472572327, 1.5610932111740112, 0.9371353983879088, -0.5861517190933228, -1.0093910694122314, -0.05471520870923996, -0.14586205780506134, 0.7224563360214233, -0.13289760053157806, -0.15394732356071472, -0.9268101453781128, -0.8129943609237671, -1.178029179573059, 1.2854552268981934, -0.6680548191070557, 0.6224685907363892, 1.312169909477234, 0.18481019139289856, -0.7934204936027527, 0.6427496671676636, -0.784182071685791, 0.2003803849220276, 0.2407369613647461, 0.6718747615814209, -0.7335630655288696, 1.1666924953460691, 0.03396851569414139, 0.6685264110565186, -0.650993824005127, -0.9513992071151732, 0.14552149176597595, 0.7318062782287598, 0.448978066444397, 0.2244233638048172, 0.9707750082015992, 0.41940855979919434, -0.8297429084777832, 1.805640697479248, 1.9366047382354736, -0.3383622467517853, 1.3287382125854492, 0.9136728644371032, 0.1604611575603485, 0.9336696863174438, 0.4585455656051636, -0.4083913266658783, 0.05308336019515991, -0.584978461265564, 0.06374193727970123, 0.46464887261390686, 0.6495269536972046, -0.2780296504497528, 0.1954038441181183, -0.6284229755401611, -0.8183373808860779, -0.45727264881134033, 1.1865794658660889, 0.5773198008537292, 0.09530366212129592, 1.3229371309280396, 1.4569662809371948, 0.7591184973716736, -0.8428829908370972, -0.44168123602867126, -0.18194180727005005, 0.1194615587592125, 1.5544248819351